5. JavaScript Engines
A JavaScript engine is responsible for executing JavaScript code.

Different browsers use different JavaScript engines.
Browser	JavaScript Engine
Chrome	V8 (Open Source)
Firefox	SpiderMonkey
Safari	JavaScriptCore
Internet Explorer	Chakra (Proprietary)

Important:
- Different browsers use different JavaScript engines.
- JavaScript engines execute JavaScript code.
- The engine is an important component of the browser.

6. Characteristics of JavaScript
1. Client-side scripting language.
2. Dynamically typed language.
3. Weakly typed language.
4. Interpreted language.
5. Synchronous in nature (Single-threaded).

6.1 Client-side Scripting Language
- JavaScript can run inside a web browser.
- No need for a server to process every action.
- It can directly change buttons, text and images.
Example: When we click a button, the text on the webpage can change without reloading the page.

9. JavaScript Runtime Environment (JRE)
JavaScript Runtime Environment provides all the necessary components to run JavaScript code.
- JavaScript Engine executes JavaScript code.
- JavaScript initially performs synchronous operations.
- It can handle asynchronous operations using Web APIs and the Event Loop.
Components of JavaScript Runtime Environment
1. JavaScript Engine
2. Web APIs / Browser APIs
3. Call Stack
4. Memory Heap
5. Garbage Collection
6. Event Loop
7. Microtask Queue
8. Task Queue
JavaScript Engine
JavaScript Engine consists of:
- JIT Compiler: Just-In-Time Compiler.
- Memory Heap: Stores objects and dynamically allocated memory.
- Call Stack: Manages function execution.
- Garbage Collection: Removes unused memory and helps free memory.
Execution Flow
- Synchronous code executes first.
- Asynchronous operations are handled by Web APIs.
- After completion, callbacks are sent to the appropriate queues.
- Event Loop checks whether the Call Stack is empty.
- Microtask Queue gets priority over Task Queue.
- Once the Call Stack is empty, callbacks are moved to the Call Stack for execution.
Important:
- Call Stack follows LIFO (Last In, First Out).
- JavaScript executes synchronous code one by one.
- Event Loop helps JavaScript handle asynchronous operations.
10. Ways to Run JavaScript Code (Environment Setup)
JavaScript code can run in two main environments:
1. Browser
2. Node.js
10.1 Browser Environment
- JavaScript code is executed with the help of a browser.
- JavaScript completely depends on the browser environment when using browser APIs.
- Browser provides Web APIs to execute browser-related operations.
Examples: Chrome, Firefox, Safari.
10.2 Node.js Environment
- Node.js is a JavaScript runtime environment.
- It allows us to execute JavaScript code outside the browser.
- It provides multiple runtime APIs.
- We can execute JavaScript code without opening a browser.
Important:
- Node.js is not a programming language.
- Node.js is a JavaScript runtime environment.
- It uses the V8 JavaScript engine.
Difference Between Browser and Node.js
Browser	Node.js
Executes JavaScript inside a browser.	Executes JavaScript outside the browser.
Provides Browser APIs.	Provides Node.js APIs.
Used mainly for frontend development.	Used mainly for backend development.
Can access DOM.	Does not provide the browser DOM by default.

11. Ways to Add / Implement JavaScript Code in Browser
There are 2 ways to add JavaScript code to HTML.
11.1 Inline JavaScript
- Writing JavaScript code directly inside an HTML element.
- We use event attributes such as onclick.
- Code is written inside the HTML tag.
Example:
<button onclick="alert('Hello')">Click Me</button>


11.2 Internal JavaScript
- JavaScript code is written in the same HTML file.
- We use the <script> tag.
- The script tag is generally placed at the end of the body tag.
- It uses paired tags (opening and closing tags).
Example:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>

<script>
    console.log("Hello World");
</script>

</body>
</html>


11.3 External JavaScript
- JavaScript code is written in another file outside the HTML file.
- The file extension is .js.
- We connect the JavaScript file using the src attribute of the script tag.
- The script tag can be placed at the end of the body tag.
Example:

HTML file:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<body>

<script src="script.js"></script>

</body>
</html>


JavaScript file (script.js):
console.log("Hello World");

6.2 Dynamically Typed Language
- No need to define the variable type (number, string, etc.).
- JavaScript automatically detects the type at runtime.
- We can assign different data types to the same variable.

6.3 Weakly Typed Language
- Allows operations between different data types.
- JavaScript automatically converts data types in some operations.
- It may cause unexpected behaviour.

6.4 Interpreted Language
- Executes code line by line during execution.
- No need to manually compile each line of code.
- Easier to debug.
- Code execution and error identification are generally easier compared to traditional compiled languages.

6.5 Synchronous in Nature (Single-threaded)
- JavaScript is synchronous by default.
- It executes one task at a time.
- It follows the call stack.
- It can cause delays if one task takes too long.
- JavaScript can also handle asynchronous operations.
Synchronous: Executes one task at a time.

Asynchronous: Allows certain operations to happen without blocking the execution of other code.
